          "id": "m_0i46f05",
          "actor": "ai",
          "provider": "openrouter",
          "model": "openai/gpt-5.4",
          "type": "ai_answer",
          "text": "Their ablation study shows that **both parts of BERT pre-training matter, but bidirectional masked-LM pre-training matters most**.\n\n- **Removing NSP** (“No NSP”) causes small-to-moderate drops vs. BERT\\(_\\text{BASE}\\), especially on **QNLI, MNLI, and SQuAD**: e.g. SQuAD F1 drops from **88.5 \\(\\to\\) 87.9**.\n- **Replacing bidirectional MLM with a left-to-right LM** (“LTR & No NSP”) hurts **all tasks more strongly**, with especially large drops on **MRPC** (**86.7 \\(\\to\\) 77.5**) and **SQuAD** (**88.5 \\(\\to\\) 77.8**).\n- **Adding a BiLSTM on top** of the LTR model helps SQuAD somewhat (**77.8 \\(\\to\\) 84.9**) but still stays well below BERT, and it **hurts GLUE tasks** further.\n\nSo the takeaway is: **NSP helps, but deep bidirectional pre-training is the key reason BERT works so well.**",
